WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.500
From heists-within-heists to shocking&nbsp;
betrayals, we're here to untangle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.883
this series' many complex knots. This is the&nbsp;
ending of "Kaleidoscope" Season 1, explained.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.820
Episodes of Netflix's dazzling heist drama&nbsp;
"Kaleidoscope" can be viewed in any order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.520
apart from "White," which should always be watched&nbsp;
last. While the show's non-linear format initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.380
seems like just a cool gimmick, it soon reveals&nbsp;
itself to be an elegant feat of storytelling.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.340
Luckily, "Kaleidoscope" isn't likely&nbsp;
to be "one last job" for its makers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.020
and it's certainly far from their first.&nbsp;
Show creator Eric Garcia wrote the novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.480
"Matchstick Men," which was adapted into&nbsp;
the 2003 movie of the same name. That film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.700
was directed by Ridley Scott, who serves as an&nbsp;
executive producer on "Kaleidoscope." Notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.980
"Matchstick Men," which stars Nicolas Cage and&nbsp;
Alison Lohman as a father-daughter con artist duo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.540
plays tricks on its characters and the&nbsp;
audience, to entertaining and emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.440
effect. Obviously, this is more than a&nbsp;
little reminiscent of "Kaleidoscope."

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.480
Garcia and Scott aren't the only intriguing&nbsp;
figures involved in "Kaleidoscope." Leading man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.600
Ray Vernon is fantastically played by Giancarlo&nbsp;
Esposito. As elegant criminal mastermind Gus Fring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.580
on "Better Call Saul" and "Breaking Bad," he works&nbsp;
hard to protect a super-secure underground lair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.560
rather than breach one. On the opposite end&nbsp;
of the show's call sheet is Max Casella,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.660
who stars in the "Pink" episode as small-time&nbsp;
crook and big-time podcast therapy fan Taco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.540
who steals just about every scene he's in.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:32.880
Stan Loomis, played by Peter Mark Kendall, is&nbsp;
a smuggler who loves the finer things in life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.160
and lacks a taste for personal growth or even&nbsp;
basic heist hygiene. More than once, Stan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.440
distractible, dreamy nature leads to ruin for the&nbsp;
crew. Most notably, he leaves his hipster glasses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.300
at the scene of the crime and makes a getaway&nbsp;
in a car with easily traceable vanity plates.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.940
Though he's one of the least&nbsp;
violent members of the crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.800
Stan indirectly spills his share of blood.&nbsp;
Carlos takes out Stan's mother and wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.140
and Stan dodges the feds when they take down&nbsp;
Bob. By the end of "Kaleidoscope," Stan has also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.880
butchered any chance he once had to learn from&nbsp;
his mistakes. Still, he lives to try another day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:11.158
and stuff his face with tacos when he realizes&nbsp;
Judy has likely taken the money and run.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:16.860
A bent lawyer who moonlights as a fence, Ava&nbsp;
Mercer is Agent Nazan Abbasi's white whale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:22.920
To protect Ray after he escapes from prison, Ava&nbsp;
notifies the FBI that Nazan is using drugs on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.500
job. This costs Nazan custody of her kid and&nbsp;
kickstarts her quest for revenge against Ava.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.960
Nazan's obsession with Ava and Ray comes to a&nbsp;
fatal head in "Pink" and dead-ends in "White."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.800
Ava's motives aren't always entirely&nbsp;
clear outside of her fondness for Ray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.460
her grandmother, and the high life.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, Nazan's obsession with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:47.100
tying Ava and Ray to the Diamond Way heist and&nbsp;
the SLS job is simple. The lady wants revenge.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.020
Ultimately, Nazan gets a version of this,&nbsp;
as Ava is killed in a shoot-out after a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:57.480
bitter fight for her life. But Nazan doesn't&nbsp;
live long enough to enjoy it. As Roger warns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.860
Hannah in "Yellow," the Triplets take Nazan&nbsp;
down by sending a lackey to take her out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.360
the street. She dies a few steps later.&nbsp;
This is just another way "Kaleidoscope"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.838
drives home the idea that chasing white&nbsp;
whales only leads to death by drowning.

00:03:10.838 --> 00:03:15.480
Bob and Judy Goodwin's story shows how&nbsp;
hard it is to escape a toxic romance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:20.100
a life of crime. Essentially, Bob is&nbsp;
fire, Judy is gasoline, and together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:25.500
they burn down any trust left among the crew on&nbsp;
the night of the heist. This comes to a head when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.700
Bob attempts to steal the full seven billion&nbsp;
for them and take out the rest of the team.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.560
“You did the right thing, okay?”

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.420
While Judy appears to end her relationship with&nbsp;
Bob in "White" by seemingly killing him to save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.520
Stan, we see her heart still belongs to him in&nbsp;
"Pink." "Kaleidoscope" knows old habits don't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.920
die hard, they die twice. Bob meets an end that&nbsp;
awaits so many fueled by jealousy, one he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.538
have avoided by going to therapy before he's set&nbsp;
on a one-way path to bleeding out on the beach.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.120
Though RJ is great with mechanics and music, the&nbsp;
crew's getaway driver doesn't have much to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.420
aside from making the occasional&nbsp;
funny comment and shooting Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:05.220
in the buttock. Despite having a sweet,&nbsp;
winning energy, RJ still loses big time.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:10.140
Crime isn't in RJ's nature, but he fights&nbsp;
to prove himself to Ray and the crew. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.040
of the central questions "Kaleidoscope"&nbsp;
asks is whether anyone can really change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.600
and if it's worth it. RJ changes from a&nbsp;
fairly good dude to a hardened criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.040
by drawing a gun on Bob. However, he can't&nbsp;
quite make the tough guy transition stick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.440
especially since RJ's trying to protect Judy,&nbsp;
who kills him to protect Bob. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.160
he's just too good for the heist&nbsp;
game, and way too bad at playing it.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.320
On a happier note, Hannah's sister Liz survives&nbsp;
the emotional ending of "Kaleidoscope." Like RJ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.740
we don't know much about Liz, but we&nbsp;
know she's comfortable enough with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.120
crime to manage to keep her nose clean.&nbsp;
However, RJ might have gotten off easy if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.140
Liz traded her life of leisure for a life of&nbsp;
helping Hannah manage the Triplets' wealth.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:55.080
With cheekbones that good, Roger has got to&nbsp;
be bad. Most episodes portray Roger as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.000
well-heeled and respected creator of the world's&nbsp;
safest safe, but that couldn't be further from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.380
truth. Roger is the latest long con pulled&nbsp;
by Ray Vernon's former partner in crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:09.360
Graham Davies. If Ray's story explores how&nbsp;
poisonous a life fueled by revenge can be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.580
Roger's is a cautionary tale confirming there's&nbsp;
no rest for the wicked. Though Roger enjoys the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.680
high life, he's always looking over his shoulder,&nbsp;
even before Leo and his crew arrive on the scene.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.660
Roger admits to Ray that he isn't sure he could've&nbsp;
saved Lily. He tells Hannah that loyalty means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.800
nothing to him. But there's something bittersweet&nbsp;
in just how much he protests against connection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:34.500
while also blatantly seeking it. But as his wife&nbsp;
leaves him and he loses his son to what may one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.838
day be a murder charge, it's easy to see his&nbsp;
treasure for what it is, and that's fools' gold.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.880
Hannah is Ray's daughter and Leo's&nbsp;
greatest protégé. She's warm, intelligent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.040
and inscrutable when it comes to where her&nbsp;
allegiance lies. Until "Pink," that is, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.180
she reveals to her father that she took the take&nbsp;
and switched the bearer bonds with copy paper.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.680
Cynics will say Hannah plays her dad to save&nbsp;
her own neck from the Triplets. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.420
her heartfelt admission to Ray makes it clear that&nbsp;
she pulls the con to save her dad from himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:08.400
By switching the bonds out before the crew can&nbsp;
make off with them, she protects the evil trio's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.560
fortune and buys back her dad's safety. She also&nbsp;
manages to purchase a super-sweet mansion to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:18.960
in with her baby and Ava's dogs. Hannah breaks&nbsp;
the family cycle of self-sabotage, bonds with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.640
estranged dad, and seems to be the only member of&nbsp;
the "Kaleidoscope" heist crew to come out on top.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.260
"I’m not your sweetpea, and&nbsp;
I’m not your inside man.”

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:33.420
It all seems peachy until you consider the&nbsp;
international bad guys who now run Hannah's life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:37.980
and the fact that her dad is likely murdered&nbsp;
by Roger's kid. If these sobering realities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.956
don't take the sweetness out of that&nbsp;
seaside mansion, we don't know what will.

00:06:42.233 --> 00:06:47.100
The side criminals of "Kaleidoscope" aren't&nbsp;
exactly gentlemen thieves like Ray, but they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.080
horrifying, haughty, and hilarious. Plus, they&nbsp;
all meet absolutely awful ends that illustrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.180
a key "Kaleidoscope" theme. Basically, if you do&nbsp;
crime long enough, you'll definitely take a fall.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.780
Carlos is Roger's right-hand&nbsp;
man, ready to throw hands and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.980
guns whenever the need arises. Though this&nbsp;
hitman picks off his fair share of lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.540
he meets his bitter end in "Red." Then&nbsp;
there's Andrew, a former employee of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:14.580
Roger's who is framed by Hannah and engages in&nbsp;
his own crimes of extortion after Roger fires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:19.500
him. "Kaleidoscope" demonstrates that greed is&nbsp;
definitely not good many times over, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:24.120
when cocky Andrew overplays his hand and gets&nbsp;
a plastic bag to the head for his trouble.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:29.340
Perhaps the most memorable side criminals of&nbsp;
all are the therapy podcast-loving Taco and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:34.440
cardigan-rocking Samson. This dynamic duo&nbsp;
provides a brief but potent shot of belly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.057
laughter alongside their bloodshed&nbsp;
during their brief time on screen.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.200
"Kaleidoscope" is about cycles, whether it's&nbsp;
the turn of the screw, the tumble of locks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.520
or the spin of the titular kaleidoscope.&nbsp;
These characters circle the truth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:51.660
themselves through each choice they make,&nbsp;
then endure the full-circle consequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:57.180
and occasionally, the benefits. At the center&nbsp;
of these overlapping cycles is Ray Vernon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:03.420
alias Leo Pap. Cycles are nearly impossible to&nbsp;
break, and Ray is the foremost symbol of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.920
theme. Many episodes are framed by his attempts&nbsp;
to express to Hannah why he's done what he's done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.840
and why so much of it is motivated by his&nbsp;
desire to change, or at least to redeem himself.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:18.360
It's never been about the money for Ray. Instead,&nbsp;
it's about revenge, which he gets at a grave cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.740
When the one silver lining of your perfect heist&nbsp;
is that your daughter heisted it out from under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.720
you to save your life, it's a sign that times&nbsp;
are hard. Ray loses a lot over the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:33.660
"Kaleidoscope," from the bonds to his crew to Ava,&nbsp;
and ultimately his own life. Yet he still wins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:39.180
He may be driven to exact revenge on Roger for not&nbsp;
saving Lily, a revenge he achieves by stashing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:44.387
club jewels in Roger's private vault. But his true&nbsp;
bounty is Hannah's forgiveness and acceptance.

00:08:44.555 --> 00:08:49.740
As the crew sails away from Manhattan and&nbsp;
into a bleak future in "Red," Ray says,

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:53.100
"No one can pinpoint the exact&nbsp;
moment it all fell apart."

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.960
The heist goes off perfectly, but&nbsp;
afterward, everything goes sideways. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.620
"Kaleidoscope" gives its crew plenty&nbsp;
of opportunities to suffer and shine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:05.640
Seeing our master criminals at their best&nbsp;
and worst creates an intriguing energy.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:11.100
As creator Eric Garcia told The New York Post,&nbsp;
he drew influence from many classic movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.440
including "Rififi" and "Pulp Fiction."&nbsp;
Like "Rififi," "White" showcases a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.580
largely nonverbal and very intricate&nbsp;
heist sequence. The first line spoken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.880
comes when Stan speaks after&nbsp;
several complex steps, saying,

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:25.860
"Well that was easy."

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.540
"Kaleidoscope" also delivers its own&nbsp;
contributions to the heist hall of fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:35.100
including Judy's press-on nail/sim&nbsp;
card hand-off and the sweetly comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:39.240
distraction of dosing the prison mess with&nbsp;
magic mushrooms. Best of all is the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.900
when Hannah heists her dad in a neat and&nbsp;
well-intentioned double cross he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.580
anticipate. The show doesn't indicate this&nbsp;
until the very last moment, which turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:52.138
feelings of deception into the pure delight&nbsp;
of seeing an excellent magic trick pulled off.

00:09:52.138 --> 00:09:55.138
“We may have made a little change to the plan.”

00:09:55.138 --> 00:10:01.380
Obsession, revenge, trust, and family loyalty fuel&nbsp;
the series and pack it to the brim with emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:06.060
heat. The biggest weapon in its arsenal is a&nbsp;
double-barreled shotgun of revenge and regret.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:10.500
Because of the kaleidoscope nature of the show's&nbsp;
storytelling, we get to see its many characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.660
shift throughout time. They change dramatically,&nbsp;
creating a vivid portrait of their motivations.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:19.920
Maintaining entertainment and emotional&nbsp;
resonance at the same time is hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:23.700
pull off. It's an even neater trick to&nbsp;
balance these two forces while creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:28.140
show that can reportedly be watched more&nbsp;
than 5,000 different ways. In a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:32.700
pulling this off is pulling off a magnificent&nbsp;
heist while telling a story about heists. If a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.920
big, splashy, heist-centric TV show can deftly&nbsp;
tackle destructive cycles, unspeakable trauma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.700
and the difficult healing process, there&nbsp;
may be hope for streaming media yet.

